36,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"It seems we are in a precarious position regarding personnel. We are losing key internal knowledge, hiring is slow, and previous training didn't stick with the depot supervisors. We need a vendor that provides strong, lasting support.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"The depot situation is rough. Our gear is outdated, and a general tool failed because people went back to spreadsheets. Plus, we can't afford a cutover during peak season—it's a total disaster. We need something rugged and flexible.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The financial data is telling. Capital budgets are being cut, but operating costs are ignored. More importantly, separate licenses and add-ons have seen yearly price hikes, while bundled agreements remained stable. We must avoid fragmented pricing.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, @Misaki, @Kenji, it's worse than I thought! Last winter, we lost two days because four different suppliers just pointed fingers at each other. Plus, our project scopes never last a year before they need rewriting. We need one clear point of contact!","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Misaki"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings confirm a troubling pattern. We have a history of neglecting critical upgrades due to resource constraints and suffering long-term errors from external data migrations. We cannot afford another ""black box"" transition or a system that stagnates.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Yumi, @Benjamin, it sounds like we are all seeing a pattern of fragmentation and instability. If we can't hire admins and the depot staff ignore standard training, a complex or rigid system will fail.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Yumi"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Misaki, exactly! If the gear is old and the supervisors do their own thing, we can't just force a fancy system on them. It has to actually work in the yard, or they'll just use spreadsheets again.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Yumi, your point about finger-pointing is critical. Combined with the price hikes on separate licenses, it's clear that a modular approach with multiple contracts is a financial and operational liability. We need a bundled, stable cost structure.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, I totally agree! Having one person to call and one bill to pay would be so much better. We can't have another winter where nobody knows who is in charge while everything is broken!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we are in agreement that fragmentation is our primary enemy. Whether it is the financial bleed of separate licenses or the technical decay of neglected upgrades, a disjointed approach is a recipe for failure.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, I agree. Given the difficulty in hiring administrators, we cannot rely on a system that requires constant, high-level internal maintenance or a complex setup that the depot crews will simply ignore.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Misaki, that's a huge point. If the people on the floor aren't buying in and we don't have the staff to help them, we need a vendor who actually stays on-site to make it work.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Kenji, on-site presence is valuable, but only if the pricing is predictable. We cannot afford a solution that looks cheap today but introduces recurring, escalating costs through separate modules or hidden fees.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, you're so right! And @Kenji, if they stay on-site, maybe they can help us keep the plan updated so it doesn't fall apart the moment a new account arrives! We need a partner, not just a product.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. We require a solution that avoids the pitfalls of external ""lift and shift"" failures and the stagnation of on-premise neglect. A partner who ensures the system evolves with us, rather than aging in place, is essential.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Misaki,,We need a vendor that balances a stable cost structure with a high level of hands-on support and training that actually sticks. A solution that is too modular or too detached will likely fail our people.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm with you. We need a vendor who understands that the yard doesn't stop for a project and who will actually be there to make sure the gear and the people are in sync.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I agree. To summarize, we must prioritize a bundled pricing model to avoid escalating costs and a single point of accountability to prevent the operational chaos we have experienced. A fragmented system is a risk we cannot afford.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Yumi,,I'm totally on board! Let's find a vendor who takes full responsibility and stays flexible. I can't wait to see the candidates and pick the one that will actually keep us all smiling!,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we have reached a consensus on our requirements: a unified cost structure, a single point of accountability, and a deployment strategy that ensures long-term viability over mere initial installation. I am ready to evaluate the candidates.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
